From 5959534a3e291082ebd8b904af3ccc0ea77fa641 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Tim 'mithro' Ansell <me@mith.ro>
Date: Mon, 28 Sep 2026 01:06:38 +0930
Subject: [PATCH] dwc2: device (peripheral) mode, and the dwc2-gadget USB
 device

The DWC2 model was host-only: the device-mode registers read as zero.
Model the peripheral side as the stock Linux dwc2 driver uses it on the
BCM2835 (buffer DMA; the core has no descriptor DMA), and present the
guest's gadget to a QEMU USB host controller as a USB device:

  -device usbip-server,id=usbip0,chardev=... -device dwc2-gadget,bus=usbip0.0

The OTG connector (otg-cable property):
- "host" (A-plug) keeps today's behaviour; "device" (B-plug) makes the
  controller a peripheral; "auto" (default) is device when a dwc2-gadget
  exists for the controller, so existing command lines are unchanged.
- GINTSTS.CurMod follows GUSBCFG.ForceHstMode/ForceDevMode, else the ID
  pin (GOTGCTL.ConIDSts); GOTGINT is write-1-to-clear and GINTSTS.OTGInt
  follows it.
- The session follows the host port's VBUS (usb_port_set_vbus): B-session
  valid and SessReqInt when a host appears, SesEndDet when it goes, and
  the core then reports its bus suspended (DSTS.SuspSts).
- The gadget is attached to the host only while the guest pulls up
  (DCTL.SftDiscon clear) in device role with a valid session.

The device side:
- DCFG, DCTL (soft disconnect, global IN/OUT NAK with GINNakEff and
  GOUTNakEff), DSTS (enumerated speed, SOF frame number), DIEPMSK,
  DOEPMSK, DAINT, DAINTMSK, DIEPEMPMSK, and per endpoint DxEPCTL (EPEna,
  EPDis with EPDisbld, SNAK/CNAK, data PID, stall), DxEPINT, DxEPTSIZ,
  DxEPDMA, DTXFSTS; DPTXFSIZn with the BCM2835's per-FIFO maximum depths
  as reset values (512 words for FIFOs 1-5, 768 for 6-7), which the
  driver checks its g-tx-fifo-size against.
- A host bus reset raises USBRst, EnumDone 10 ms later (T_DRST) with
  DSTS.EnumSpd, and allows the first SETUP only after the 10 ms of reset
  recovery (T_RSTRCY): the driver re-initialises EP0 in its reset and
  enumeration handlers.  Reset signalling on a suspended bus raises
  WkUpInt, which brings the driver back from the disconnected state.
- Control transfers: the SETUP is DMA'd into EP0 OUT when armed
  (DOEPINT0.Setup, SUPCnt), then the data and status stages follow the
  guest's EP0 IN/OUT programming; a stall on EP0 answers the stage the
  host is in.
- Data endpoints: host packets wait (USB_RET_ASYNC) until the guest arms
  the endpoint, then move by DMA packet by packet with the core's
  XferSize/PktCnt accounting, so short and zero-length packets end
  transfers where they would on the wire and one host transfer may span
  several guest transfers (or the reverse).  An endpoint the guest has
  not activated stalls.  Isochronous endpoints move one packet per
  (micro)frame without waiting.  Activating an endpoint publishes its
  type to QEMU's USB core (usb_ep_set_type).

Tested with the stock Raspberry Pi Zero W kernel (6.18.52+, dwc2 module,
dtoverlay=dwc2 applied with fdtoverlay) on raspi0, a configfs gadget
bound, and a USB/IP client as the host: CDC-ACM (a shell on ttyGS0),
CDC-ECM and CDC-NCM (ARP and ICMP echo with usb0), mass storage (reads
of the gadget's image, a write the guest then sees in its file), a
composite of ACM + ECM + mass storage; no host at all, a host at boot,
a host after boot, detach and reattach.  The driver probes as on
silicon ("EPs: 8, dedicated fifos, 4080 entries in SPRAM") with no
parameter warnings.  Host mode is unchanged (raspi0 usb-net, raspi4b).

